    "body": "## Motivation\n\n`update_weights_from_disk(recapture_cuda_graph=True)` only recaptures decode graphs. When loading replaces parameter storage, target prefill graphs still replay the old addresses: they can serve stale weights or access freed memory even though the update succeeds.\n\n## Modifications\n\nRoute weight updates through a callback that cleans up and recaptures an existing target prefill graph before recapturing decode, matching startup order. Use a fresh shared pool so prefill-only recapture does not revive a drained pool and hit `CachingHostAllocator`'s `use_count > 0` assertion. Keep the existing speculative draft placement guard; spec-worker-owned draft graph recapture is outside this change. The broader backend cleanup work in #44296 is separate.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nReproduced on H100, PyTorch 2.14.1+cu130, baseline `a99c7d050f`, using Qwen3-0.6B and real HTTP generation/weight-update requests. This is a **controlled parameter-storage reallocation repro**, not a claim that the ordinary Qwen loader reallocates weights:\n\n- Created checkpoint B by negating checkpoint A's MLP down-projection weights. A probe wrapper clones parameter storage before reload and retains the previous storage, making stale reads deterministic.\n- Before the fix, with breakable prefill graphs and decode graphs disabled, all 6 prompts retained A's first token and logprob after updating to B. None matched a fresh eager B server. A second update released the original storage and hit an illegal memory access in prefill.\n- After the fix, tested two consecutive updates with decode graphs disabled and with full decode graphs enabled: all 24 post-update requests matched the fresh eager B server's complete 8-token outputs and token logprobs exactly (maximum difference 0), with no failed requests. Server logs confirm prefill graph replay.\n- Existing draft recapture tests: 3 passed. Ruff lint/format and `git diff --check` passed. No new test files.\n\nGPT-OSS-20B/Marl